Milestone 5 numerical contract, version 1
========================================

This contract is established before Metal/AVX2 implementation. Committed
tolerances are part of the numerical contract; do not relax them to pass a kernel.
End-to-end design section 9 thresholds remain a separate requirement.

Interface and layout
--------------------
src/backend.h preserves the design's vtable. One request is one layer and
1..8 tokens; every token supplies ten distinct routed ids, eleven nonnegative
finite gates (shared expert 512 last), and 2560 finite fp32 activations.
The data plane will decode fp16 before calling the backend. There is no
additional fp16 rounding here. Output is fp32, with inactive tokens zeroed.

load borrows a complete immutable 3,404,021,760-byte ownership block until
unload. The caller must keep the buffer alive and validate its file hash.
Within it, order is layer, expert, projection (gate/up/down), row, Q4 group.
Each projection occupies 46,080 bytes. Gate/up are 32x2560; down is 2560x32.
No requantization or transpose occurs. Calls are externally serialized;
run has no allocation or I/O and leaves output unchanged on failure.
No loaded blocks is a state error, not a successful zero partial.

Each Q4_0 group is an unaligned little-endian fp16 scale and 16 packed bytes.
Byte j's low nibble is weight j; high nibble is weight j+16. Weight=(q-8)*d;
negative and subnormal scales are valid, nonfinite scales are rejected when
read (including experts with zero gates). The canonical layout was verified
against ggml's quantize_row_q4_0_ref/dequantize_row_q4_0:
https://github.com/ggml-org/ggml/blob/master/src/ggml-quants.c
Verified on 2026-10-28. This defines a byte decoder, not the milestone 10
production quantizer.

Oracle arithmetic
-----------------
ref decodes weights exactly, multiplies fp32 operands, and accumulates every
dot in double in column order. Gate/up dots round to fp32. Stable double SiLU
and multiplication produce an fp32 hidden activation. Each down dot, routing
multiply and sum across experts/blocks use double; only final output rounds
to fp32. Order is token, routed expert (request order), shared expert, ascending
owned block, dimension. Nonfinite inputs/results and fp32 overflow fail.
FE_TONEAREST is required; no global rounding mode is changed. Ref is permitted
to read weights token-first; optimized backends must reuse reads across burst.

ref-q8 differs only by quantizing x and each owned 32-neuron hidden slice
before its dot. Symmetric signed Q8 uses maxabs/127 fp32 scale, 127/maxabs
fp32 multiplier, nearest-even rounding, clamp [-127,127], then fp32 dequant.
Input groups have 64 elements; each hidden slice has 32 (zero-padding to 64
would give the same result). Zero groups stay zero; subnormal groups whose
scale underflows become zero. Overflowing reciprocal uses x/maxabs*127;
dequantization is capped at +/-FLT_MAX if rounded scale would overflow.
This models the preserved Shuttle activation scheme. It does NOT certify
that archive's custom Q4 weight format as canonical Q4_0: milestone 7 retains
the explicit compatibility gate. No accelerated code is ported here.

Comparison
----------
For EACH token's partial, not an average over a burst or the complete model:
  relative_l2 = sqrt(sum((actual-ref)^2) / sum(ref^2))
  normalized_max = max(abs(actual-ref)) / max(abs(ref))
  cosine = dot(actual,ref) / sqrt(sum(actual^2)*sum(ref^2))
All reductions use double. All three limits must pass inclusively. For a zero
reference only an exactly zero candidate passes (metrics 0,0,1); any nonzero
candidate has metrics infinity,infinity,0. A zero candidate with nonzero
reference has cosine 0. NaN/Inf always fail. Cosine is clamped to [-1,1] for
roundoff. Per-coordinate relative error is intentionally not used at zeros.

Frozen limits (src/backend.c), inclusive:

                         relative L2    normalized max    cosine floor
  ref reproducibility     0.000001       0.000001          0.999999999999
  backend conformance     0.025          0.030             0.9998

The second row applies to ref-q8 and future accelerated backends. No test may
rewrite goldens or tune limits automatically. If a later backend cannot pass,
stop and explain; do not loosen these limits. Unit tests exercise each boundary.

Fixtures, reproducibility and measured Q8 envelope
------------------------------------------------
src/vectors.c version-1 recipe constructs canonical Q4 bytes directly, with
fixed unsigned xorshift32 streams (13,17,5), signed finite fp16 scales, all
nibble values, subnormal scales, and fixed fp32 activations/gates. It never
quantizes/requantizes model weights. The entire recipe is version controlled.
Each .txt header binds name, layer, burst, ownership mask, and a SHA-256 over
the request and every materialized weight slice. Integers and float bits in
the digest are explicitly little-endian; compiler struct padding is excluded.
The rest is token-major 2560-dimensional reference output in exact C99 hex
float notation. SHA256SUMS additionally binds each complete golden file.
Missing files, changed recipes, bad hashes, nonfinite/truncated/extra data,
incorrect output metadata, or nonzero inactive tokens fail self-test.

Fixture        Layer  Burst  Owned blocks   Coverage
zero-input       0      1    0              exact zero reference
zero-gates       1      2    19             zero routed AND shared gates
signed           0      1    0              signed scales, full hidden state
reused          17      4    7              same experts across different tokens
mixed           47      8    0,19           distinct routes, layer/block/id edges
outliers        31      2    19             activation outliers vs small values
tiny            23      1    11             small nonzero reference norms
all-blocks       1      1    0..19          complete neuron ownership

All fixtures include the shared expert; ids 0 and 511 are covered. The corpus
has 20 token partials (51,200 fp32 outputs), 17 nonzero and three exactly zero.
Sparse anonymous mappings exercise real >2GiB offsets without model downloads
or multi-GB resident allocations: only the requested layer and expert slices
are populated. The all-blocks case reserves about 68GB VIRTUAL address space,
but materializes about 30.4MB of weights. No swap/disk weights are written.

Generate explicitly into a NEW empty directory (existing files are refused):
  make build/fronk-vectors
  mkdir build/new-vectors
  ./build/fronk-vectors generate build/new-vectors
  ./build/fronk-vectors check ref tests/vectors
  ./build/fronk-vectors check ref-q8 tests/vectors
  make test-q4 test-backend test-vectors

Ordinary tests never regenerate or update goldens. Compare regenerated files
and SHA256SUMS byte-for-byte when auditing reproducibility; do not replace the
committed corpus implicitly. The sanitizer generator is build/test/fronk-vectors.
Its initial output and an independent -O2 generation matched byte-for-byte.
Self-test temporarily loads/unloads fixtures, requires idle backend state,
and releases fixtures on failure. It can be reused by the future SELFTEST
control command without running it inside the current control event loop.

Initial Q8 measurements, 2026-10-28, M4 Pro, Apple clang 21, ASan/UBSan:
  maximum relative L2:      0.0123886178168 (mixed token 2)
  maximum normalized max:   0.0143968827672 (signed token 0)
  minimum cosine:          0.999923474904  (mixed token 2)
Per-token observations are in q8-baseline.txt; all zero references were exact.
The L2 and maximum-error limits provide respectively 2.018x and 2.084x the
observed maxima. The cosine deficit budget, 1-0.9998, is 2.614x the observed
worst deficit. This is a measured synthetic envelope, not evidence of real-model
accuracy; section 9 validation remains mandatory. Rosetta x86_64 passed the
same committed corpus/limits, with no architecture-specific tolerance changes.

Independent evidence beyond generated expectations: test_q4 checks all 65,536
fp16 encodings, nibble basis vectors, group offsets and Q8 nearest-even ties.
test_backend compares ref to an independently expanded dense-matrix oracle,
checks analytic SiLU(1), routed/shared gate scaling and block additivity,
read-only mapped weights, all burst lengths, and bit-identical repeated-expert
bursts versus individual tokens for BOTH reference variants. Corruption and
deliberately broken zero-output backends fail the conformance tests.
